  "kind": "divisibility_proof",
  "title": "3 divides n³ − n",
  "prompt": "Prove: for every integer $n$, $3 \\mid (n^3 - n)$.",
  "answer_format": "A structured proof: let, case, expr and witness steps (notes optional). No final answer box. Use case steps to split into possibilities.",
  "difficulty": 3,
  "tags": [
    "cases",
    "divisibility",
    "remainders"
  ],
  "data": {
    "variables": [
      "n"
    ],
    "target": "n^3 - n",
    "goal": {
      "divisor": 3
    }
  },
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Every integer leaves remainder 0, 1 or 2 when divided by 3.",
    "Write one case for each remainder: n = 3k, n = 3k + 1, n = 3k + 2. Expand and factor out 3 in each."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Case $n = 3k$: $n^3 - n = 27k^3 - 3k = 3(9k^3 - k)$.",
    "Case $n = 3k + 1$: $n^3 - n = 27k^3 + 27k^2 + 6k = 3(9k^3 + 9k^2 + 2k)$.",
    "Case $n = 3k + 2$: $n^3 - n = 27k^3 + 54k^2 + 33k + 6 = 3(9k^3 + 18k^2 + 11k + 2)$.",
    "The three cases cover every integer, so $3 \\mid (n^3 - n)$. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "sundstrom-mr3",
    "section": "3.5 The Division Algorithm and Congruence",
    "note": "Original problem; see proofs using the possible remainders."
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "retired",
    "notes": "Invented problem, retired 2026-11-06: MATH 123 proofs now map 1:1 to Book of Proof exercises."
  },
